<misterromdescription>
    <name>Raiden Fighters 2 - Operation Hell Dive (Germany, SPI cartridge, self-flashing)</name>
    <setname>rdft2</setname>
    <rbf>SeibuSPI</rbf>
    <mameversion>0277</mameversion>
    <year>1997</year>
    <manufacturer>Seibu Kaihatsu</manufacturer>
    <players>2</players>
    <joystick>8</joystick>
    <rotation>vertical (cw)</rotation>
    <region>Germany</region>
    <category>Shooter</category>

    <!--
      THE GAME FLASHES ITSELF. This is the authentic variant: the sample flash
      ships BLANK and the cartridge's own sound ROMs come along as the material
      the game's updater reads, so on first boot it runs the real "NOW UPDATING"
      ritual -- the one with the techno music -- and programs its own samples,
      exactly as a fresh cartridge does on real hardware.

      `rdft2.mra` ships the same game with the flash already
      programmed and boots straight in. That one is what most people want; this
      one is what the hardware does.

      TWO THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE RUNNING IT.

      * The update takes tens of seconds, and at the end the game HALTS on
        "UPDATE COMPLETED. PLEASE TURN THE POWER BACK ON." Reset the core (or
        reload the MRA) and it plays. Real hardware takes minutes, because it
        has to actually program 2 MB a byte at a time; the core charges no
        program time, as MAME does not either.
      * IT SAVES, BUT NOT BY ITSELF. When the ritual finishes, OPEN THE OSD
        once: MiSTer asks the core for its nvram only while that menu is up
        (Main_MiSTer's menu.cpp, MENU_GENERIC_MAIN2), and the core has been
        waiting to hand it over since the flash went quiet. After that the save
        file loads on every boot and the ritual never runs again.

      The trigger is the game's own, not a jumper: the last four bytes of the
      386 program are stamped into flash[0..3] when it finishes, and a blank or
      mismatched stamp is what makes the updater run. So an image programmed by
      one region's cartridge is rejected by another's, which is the region lock.
    -->

    <!--
      Part order is significant. rtl/rom_loader.sv walks this list with a fixed
      table -- selected by the mod byte below -- and performs MAME's
      ROM_LOAD24_* / ROM_LOAD32_* byte scatter in hardware. `make check-mra`
      holds this file, that table and MAME's ROM_START against each other.

      The tail is where this differs from the pre-flashed MRA, and it is in
      MAME's own region order: sound01's two ROMs, then soundflash1's blank.

        part 14  the PCM source ROM      -> 0x2900000, the source window
        part 15  the second sound ROM    -> 0x0480000, source AND Z80 program
        part 16  a BLANK 2 MB flash      -> 0x0280000, what the game programs

      Layout produced in SDRAM (see rtl/spi_defs.vh):
        0x0000000  2 MB   386 program
        0x0200000  256 KB Z80 program -- RAM, NOT loaded here. The 386 fills it
                          through port 0x688 and releases the Z80 with 0x68C.
        0x0240000  192 KB text tiles
        0x0280000  2 MB   YMF271 sample flash, blank at boot
        0x0480000  512 KB the second sound ROM, packed one byte per 386 dword
        0x0500000  12 MB  background tiles
        0x1100000  18 MB  sprites, three 6 MB plane-pair chunks
        0x2900000  2 MB   the PCM source ROM, read through the sound01 window
    -->

    <!--
      Index 1 is the core's config blob. Byte 0 is the mod byte: bit 0 selects
      the SXX2C board wiring, bits 3:1 the set, and BIT 4 the authentic-flash
      tail of that set's part table plus the 386's sound01 source window. Bit 4
      alone does nothing -- it is ANDed with bit 0 in SeibuSPI.sv, because on
      SXX2E it would open a window with nothing behind it.

      IT MUST COME BEFORE THE index="0" ROM. Main_MiSTer sends <rom> elements in
      file order, and an index-1 that follows the image arrives after the loader
      has already walked its table.
    -->
    <rom index="1"><part>13</part></rom>

    <!--
      Worth noting what this variant costs rdft2 and rfjet nothing: their
      pre-flashed images are DERIVED by a decompressor (CODEC_BPE_DPCM) whose
      format had to be cracked. Here there is no derived image and no codec --
      the game's own 386 does the decompression, as it does on hardware.
    -->

    <rom index="0" address="0x30000000" zip="rdft2.zip" md5="none">
        <!-- 386 program: four byte lanes -->
        <part name="prg0.tun" crc="3cb3fdca"/>
        <part name="prg1.bin" crc="cab55d88"/>
        <part name="prg2.bin" crc="83758b0e"/>
        <part name="prg3.bin" crc="084fb5e4"/>

        <!-- text layer: three byte lanes, and the lane order is 1, 0, 2 -->
        <part name="fix0.u0524" crc="6fdf4cf6"/>
        <part name="fix1.u0518" crc="69b7899b"/>
        <part name="fixp.u0514" crc="99a5fece"/>

        <!-- background tiles: 12 MB, two D+P groups 6 MB apart -->
        <part name="bg-1d.u0535" crc="6143f576"/>
        <part name="bg-1p.u0537" crc="55e64ef7"/>
        <part name="bg-2d.u0536" crc="c607a444"/>
        <part name="bg-2p.u0538" crc="f0830248"/>

        <!-- sprites: 18 MB in three 6 MB chunks, MAME's order obj3, obj2, obj1 -->
        <part name="obj3.u0434"  crc="e08f42dc"/>
        <part name="obj3b.u0433" crc="1b6a523c"/>
        <part name="obj2.u0431"  crc="7aeadd8e"/>
        <part name="obj2b.u0432" crc="5d790a5d"/>
        <part name="obj1.u0429"  crc="c2c50f02"/>
        <part name="obj1b.u0430" crc="5259321f"/>

        <!--
          Parts 14 and 15: MAME's sound01 region, whole. sound1.u0222 is the
          same ROM the pre-flashed MRA carries twice -- once sliced and
          compressed into the flash image, once whole for the Z80 program. Here
          it is carried once, whole, and serves both purposes: the updater reads
          its compressed half through the sound01 window and the 386 reads the
          Z80 program at 0x60000 through the same window.
        -->
        <part name="pcm.u0217"    crc="2edc30b5"/>
        <part name="sound1.u0222" crc="b7bd3703"/>

        <!--
          Part 16: the sample flash, BLANK. flash0_blank_regionXX.u1053 is a
          real dump and it carries the region ID in byte 0 -- an erased or
          mismatched one is the "hardware error 81" the MAME driver header
          describes. MAME has no dump for the second E28F008SA because an
          erased chip has nothing to dump, so it is a megabyte of FF here.
        -->
        <part name="flash0_blank_region80.u1053" crc="e2adaff5"/>
        <part repeat="0x100000">FF</part>
    </rom>

    <!--
      The sample flash, saved to /media/fat/config/nvram/. Index 2 because 0 is
      the ROM image, 1 the core's config blob and 254 the DIP switches; the size
      is the two E28F008SA chips exactly. Main_MiSTer sends this file AFTER the
      index-0 image, which is what lets it land on top of the blank flash that
      image carries -- so it must stay below the <rom> element here.
    -->
    <nvram index="2" size="2097152"/>

    <!--
      Same control layout as the pre-flashed MRA; the names map to joystick bits
      4 upwards IN ORDER and SeibuSPI.sv decodes them at exactly those positions.
    -->
    <buttons names="Shot,Bomb,Button 3,Start,Coin,Service Coin,Test"
             default="A,B,X,Start,Select,R,L" count="3"/>

    <switches default="00" base="0">
        <dip name="Flip Screen"  bits="0" ids="Off,On"/>
        <dip name="Service Mode" bits="1" ids="Off,On"/>
    </switches>
</misterromdescription>
